Here are the criteria in a tracker-ready structure:

```json
{
  "page": "spring-campaign-landing",
  "acceptance_criteria": {
    "content": [
      {"criterion": "headline matches approved copy exactly", "threshold": "character-for-character match with copy doc v3"},
      {"criterion": "pricing shown", "threshold": "all 3 plans with prices matching the pricing page"},
      {"criterion": "no placeholder text or images", "threshold": "0 occurrences of lorem ipsum or placeholder URLs"}
    ],
    "performance": [
      {"criterion": "largest contentful paint on mobile", "threshold": "<= 2.5s on a mid-range Android over 4G"},
      {"criterion": "total page weight", "threshold": "<= 1.5 MB including images and fonts"}
    ],
    "accessibility": [
      {"criterion": "color contrast", "threshold": "WCAG AA, ratio >= 4.5:1 for body text"},
      {"criterion": "keyboard navigation", "threshold": "all interactive elements reachable and visibly focused"},
      {"criterion": "image alt text", "threshold": "100% of meaningful images have descriptive alt text"}
    ],
    "forms": [
      {"criterion": "invalid email handling", "threshold": "submission blocked, error message shown, no data sent"},
      {"criterion": "successful signup", "threshold": "confirmation message shown and record visible in CRM within 5 minutes"}
    ]
  }
}
```

Every criterion carries its own threshold, which is what makes the list usable at review time: 'fast' becomes 2.5 seconds on a specified device, 'accessible' becomes a named contrast ratio and full keyboard reachability. When the page is reviewed, each line is a pass or a fail with evidence, not a discussion about taste.

Here is the test plan:

```json
{
  "feature": "login form",
  "cases": [
    {
      "id": "TC-01",
      "scenario": "valid credentials",
      "steps": ["enter username 'ana.ruiz'", "enter the correct password", "submit"],
      "expected": "user is logged in and lands on the dashboard"
    },
    {
      "id": "TC-02",
      "scenario": "wrong password",
      "steps": ["enter username 'ana.ruiz'", "enter an incorrect password", "submit"],
      "expected": "generic error 'invalid username or password', no session created, attempt counted"
    },
    {
      "id": "TC-03",
      "scenario": "unknown user",
      "steps": ["enter username 'no.such.user'", "enter any password", "submit"],
      "expected": "the same generic error as TC-02, identical wording and timing, so the response does not reveal whether the account exists"
    },
    {
      "id": "TC-04",
      "scenario": "empty fields",
      "steps": ["leave username and password empty", "submit"],
      "expected": "form is not submitted; both fields flagged as required"
    },
    {
      "id": "TC-05",
      "scenario": "username case sensitivity",
      "steps": ["enter username 'Ana.Ruiz'", "enter the correct password for 'ana.ruiz'", "submit"],
      "expected": "login succeeds, usernames are treated case-insensitively as specified"
    },
    {
      "id": "TC-06",
      "scenario": "lockout after repeated failures",
      "steps": ["submit a wrong password 5 times for 'ana.ruiz'", "submit the correct password"],
      "expected": "account locked after the 5th failure; the correct password is also refused during lockout and the user is told how long the lock lasts"
    }
  ]
}
```

Two cases carry the security weight of the plan. TC-03 exists because the unknown-user response must be indistinguishable from the wrong-password response, otherwise the form becomes a tool for enumerating valid usernames. TC-06 tests the part teams forget: that lockout must also reject the correct password during the lock window, or the limit slows nobody down.

Here is the checklist:

```json
{
  "checklist": "pre-merge code review",
  "sections": {
    "correctness": [
      {"check": "the change does what the ticket describes, nothing more", "fail_if": "unrelated behavior changed without mention"},
      {"check": "edge cases handled: empty input, maximum size, invalid values", "fail_if": "any of the three crashes or silently misbehaves"},
      {"check": "error paths return or raise something the caller handles", "fail_if": "an error is swallowed with no log, message or fallback"}
    ],
    "tests": [
      {"check": "new behavior has tests that fail without the change", "fail_if": "tests pass even with the change reverted"},
      {"check": "full suite passes locally", "fail_if": "any previously passing test now fails"},
      {"check": "a regression test exists for the bug being fixed", "fail_if": "the fix has no test reproducing the original bug"}
    ],
    "security": [
      {"check": "user input is validated or parameterized at every query and output", "fail_if": "string-built SQL or unescaped output anywhere in the diff"},
      {"check": "no secrets, tokens or personal data in code, logs or tests", "fail_if": "any credential-like string appears in the diff"},
      {"check": "permission checks present on every new endpoint", "fail_if": "an endpoint relies on the caller being trusted"}
    ],
    "readability": [
      {"check": "names say what things are and do", "fail_if": "a reviewer must read the body to learn what a function does"},
      {"check": "comments explain why, not what", "fail_if": "comments restate the code or describe removed behavior"}
    ]
  }
}
```

The fail_if column is what separates a checklist from a poster: every item names the concrete condition that blocks the merge, so two reviewers applying it reach the same verdict. The strongest item in the list is the tests one, a test that passes with the change reverted proves nothing, and asking that question out loud catches a surprising amount of theater.

